Shopify Expiring Offline Access Tokens — Complete Implementation Guide
Purpose of this file

This document explains the full Shopify expiring offline access token + refresh_token flow as implemented in the product-options-tlx Laravel/Shopify PHP app.

It is written so that:

A human developer can understand exactly where and why each piece exists.
An AI coding agent (Cursor, Claude, Copilot, etc.) can read this file alone and port the same complete flow into another Shopify app project.
Official Shopify references

Offline access tokens
Token exchange
Apr 1, 2026 — required for new public apps
Jan 1, 2027 — required for all public apps
1. Problem this solves
Old behavior (non-expiring offline tokens)
On install, Shopify gave one offline access_token that never expired.
Background jobs, webhooks, and admin API calls reused that token forever.
If leaked, the token stayed valid indefinitely.
New behavior (expiring offline tokens)
When requesting with expiring=1, Shopify returns:

Field	Meaning	Typical lifetime
access_token	Used for Admin REST/GraphQL API	~60 minutes (expires_in: 3600)
refresh_token	Used only to mint a new access token	~90 days (refresh_token_expires_in: 7776000)
expires_in	Seconds until access token expires	3600
refresh_token_expires_in	Seconds until refresh token expires	7776000
scope	Granted scopes	—
Symptom if refresh is not implemented
After ~1 hour, Admin API calls fail with:

[API] Invalid API key or access token (unrecognized login or wrong password)
That happened in this project because tokens were stored, but almost nothing refreshed them before reuse.

2. High-level architecture (required mental model)
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  ACQUIRE (install / merchant opens app)                         │
│  A) OAuth authorization code + expiring=1                       │
│  B) Token exchange (session JWT → offline token) + expiring=1 │
│     Persist: access_token, refresh_token, expires_at,           │
│              refresh_token_expires_at                           │
└───────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────┘
                                │
                                ▼
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  USE (every Admin API call: HTTP, jobs, webhooks)               │
│  Before using access_token:                                     │
│    if expires within refresh buffer (e.g. 5 min)                │
│      → POST grant_type=refresh_token                            │
│      → save NEW access_token + NEW refresh_token                │
│    then call Shopify Admin API                                  │
└───────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────┘
                                │
                                ▼
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  RECOVER (merchant is present)                                  │
│  If refresh fails / refresh_token expired:                      │
│    → token exchange with App Bridge session JWT                 │
│    OR redirect to /api/auth?shop=... for full OAuth             │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
Critical Shopify rules you must follow
Always request expiring=1 when acquiring offline tokens (OAuth code exchange and token exchange).
Always store the new refresh_token returned by every refresh. Old refresh tokens are invalidated after use.
One refreshable offline token per app + shop. Acquiring a new expiring offline token retires older ones (old refresh token becomes invalid immediately; old access token may work until its expires_in).
Refresh does not need the merchant present. Token exchange / OAuth does.
If the 90-day refresh token expires, the merchant must reopen the app so you can exchange/OAuth again.
Use config values, not raw env(), when php artisan config:cache is used in production.
